<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ziębice</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Münsterberg</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ziębice (German Münsterberg) is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-silesia">Lower Silesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wroclaw">Wrocław</a>. From 1322 it was the seat of a Piast duchy and later passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-of-podebrady">George of Poděbrady</a>, whose sons held it as dukes of Münsterberg, and from 1654 to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-auersperg">Auersperg family</a>. Prussian from 1742, it became part of Poland in 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ziebice.jpg" alt="Ziębice" width="320" height="325" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacek Halicki · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:2019_Ratusz_w_Zi%C4%99bicach_3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area became part of the emerging Polish state under its first historic ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mieszko-i">Mieszko I</a>, in the 10th century. The town was first mentioned in 1234 under the Old Polish spelling Sambice. This Polish town was probably destroyed in 1241 during the first Mongol invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. According to records, a new town under German town law followed, called Munsterberck in 1253 and Sambiz videlicet Munsterberg in 1268. It became home to a German-speaking population as a result of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostsiedlung">Ostsiedlung</a>.</p><p>As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> fragmented, the town formed part of the duchies of Silesia until 1290 and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swidnica">Świdnica</a> until 1322. It then became the capital of a small duchy of its own and remained under the rule of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piast-dynasty">Piast dynasty</a> until 1521. In 1344 the Piast dukes established a court in the town. In 1521 it passed to the Podiebrad family. The town suffered in the Hussite Wars and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, and in 1643 it was hit by an epidemic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1742 Münsterberg became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and the capital of Kreis Münsterberg. In 1871 it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> with the bulk of Silesia. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> the town returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in 1945, under a Soviet-installed communist regime, and the German population was expelled. The Polish anti-communist resistance was active in Ziębice in the following years.</p><p>Ziębice lies in Ząbkowice County, in the Lower Silesian Voivodeship of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. It has an Old Town with a preserved Market Square and the Romanesque-Gothic Saint George Basilica, listed as a Historic Monument of Poland, and it holds Poland's only Museum of Home Appliances.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ziębice” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1322 the sons of Duke Bolko I of Świdnica divided his lands, and Bolko II received <b>Ziębice</b> (Münsterberg), south of Wrocław, as a separate duchy split from the Duchy of Świdnica.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ziebice">Duchy of Ziębice</a></li></ul></section>
